    "q-108945": {
      "dom_id": "step-q108945",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=7,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[5X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[5X] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=7.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot f(5)+{6}^{2}\\cdot f(6)+{9}^{2}\\cdot f(9) \\\\  & =25(\\frac{1}{5})+36(\\frac{2}{5})+81(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}-49 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[5X] & =25\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\frac{14}{5} \\\\  & =14\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
